Partly, and two findings block the build. The L7-R design and every verb it uses already exist, and round 1 of this review cleared them (`archive/peer/2026-09-24-priorart-c73-l7r.md:198-238`). But the brief leaves out that **run 1 of this recipe has already been executed**: `tools/bench/stage_d1_l7_r.log`, started 06:56, 55 pass / 1 fail, rc=1. That run contradicts the new handle gate. I couldn't write the plan file because the Write tool is disabled in this session, so the review is here.

## PART A — the direction
- **A1 (settled already) and A2 (refuted already): nothing new.** Round 1's findings still hold (`archive/peer/2026-09-24-priorart-c73-l7r.md:198-205`). The design is Pre-decided 175 (`docs/d1-loop12-17-split-plan.md:412-454`).
- **A3 (contradicted): the handle gate against run 1 of this same recipe.**
  - The recipe gates "NO GROWTH: before-save level <= post-load baseline + 100" (`tools/recipes/stage_d1_l7_r.py:112`).
  - Round 1 accepted this gate as fixed on the evidence of the read-only census, where handles fell from 45,631 to 42,604 (`archive/peer/2026-09-24-priorart-c73-l7r.md:265`).
  - Run 1 is an editing run, and its handles went up: 45,641 after open (`stage_d1_l7_r.log:28`) and **46,120 after the work** (`:468`), which is **+479**.
  - Earlier editing stages rose further: 54,456 after the work in `stage_d1_m3a4.log:118` and 54,360 in `stage_d1_m4b.log:117`.
  - So the non-fatal handle gate is expected to fail, and a failing log triggers a peer review. Run 1 crashed at `:107` before reaching `:112`, so the gate itself has never run. The handle counts it would compare have been measured.
- **A4 (unread evidence): `tools/bench/stage_d1_l7_r.log` (run 1) is not cited.** The brief describes the build as a first run ("Fresh LabVIEW"). Two facts in that log matter for the plan:
  1. **Step 8 checks nothing for two of the six carriers.**
     - Loop tunnels #1929 and #5020 were already gone before step 8 tried to delete them (`:439`, `:442`: "retire LoopTunnel #1929 already gone", and the same for #5020).
     - Their "live consumers == 0" gates (`:424-425`) therefore ran on nodes that no longer existed, so they passed without testing anything.
     - The tunnels apparently disappeared when step 3 deleted wires w4337 and w5073. The closest earlier record is `archive/peer/2026-09-24-priorart-c71-l7-1b.md:555`, the list of old tunnels allowed to disappear.
  2. **LabVIEW reused deleted uids during the run.**
     - The new tunnel's outer wire got uid **4337**, the uid of the wire deleted in step 3 (`:125`; `:232` shows #2048's 'length' input on wire 4337 again).
     - The junk Invoke node that was purged three times got uid **1397**, the uid of deleted wire w1397 (`:64`, `:82`, `:208`).
     - This behaviour is already documented (`.claude/skills/labview-automation/references/vi-scripting.md:601`, `archive/peer/2026-09-17-d1-s3b-uid-reuse-after-delete.md:109`).
     - No current gate depends on a wire uid after the deletes: A0 runs before them, and the two edge-list gates (PC1/PC2) compare terminal uids. A future gate that looked up a wire by a deleted uid would be fooled.

## PART B — the artefact
- **B1 (already built) and B3 (helper exists): nothing new.** Round 1's findings stand (`archive/peer/2026-09-24-priorart-c73-l7r.md:227-238`), and the recipe adds no new op.
